WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.130
- There are no shortage of wacky names

00:00:02.130 --> 00:00:04.530
for alcoholic beverages,
from fuzzy navels,

00:00:04.530 --> 00:00:08.130
to sex on the beaches,
or sexes on the beach,

00:00:08.130 --> 00:00:10.950
to the NSFW AMF.

00:00:10.950 --> 00:00:13.230
I'm not sure if any of
these actually taste good

00:00:13.230 --> 00:00:16.080
or if they're just fun
to yell in a crowded bar.

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:17.910
The origins of some are obvious,

00:00:17.910 --> 00:00:19.950
while others are more contentious.

00:00:19.950 --> 00:00:22.890
Two bars in Juarez, Mexico
and one tequila distributor

00:00:22.890 --> 00:00:26.460
in California are among those
claiming to be the mastermind

00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:29.760
behind the world-famous
Margarita, which was either named

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:32.760
after a woman or a flower,
depending on who you believe.

00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:34.740
Boy, if we can't even agree

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:37.440
on the etymology of an 80 year old word,

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:40.770
how are we going to find ones
that are 5,000 years old?

00:00:40.770 --> 00:00:43.230
Fortunately, or unfortunately,

00:00:43.230 --> 00:00:45.420
humans have been enjoying
alcoholic beverages

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:47.340
since the dawn of civilization,

00:00:47.340 --> 00:00:49.440
so there's plenty of linguistic evidence

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:52.470
in many languages to
point us to the truth.

00:00:52.470 --> 00:00:54.280
As the Romans said, in vino veritas.

00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:58.811
I'm Dr. Erica Brozovsky
and this is "Other Words".

00:00:58.811 --> 00:01:01.978
(quirky upbeat music)

00:01:05.666 --> 00:01:08.820
♪ Other words ♪

00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:13.050
Speaking of vino, in 2017,
a group of archeologists

00:01:13.050 --> 00:01:16.350
discovered the earliest
known winery in the world.

00:01:16.350 --> 00:01:18.600
In an ancient village
east of the Black Sea,

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:22.080
in what is now Georgia, stone
age people were growing grapes

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:24.960
and fermenting the juice 8,000 years ago.

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:28.380
This is not far geographically
or chronologically

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:31.020
from the speakers of Proto-Indo-European,

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:33.270
a hypothetical language
that linguists believe

00:01:33.270 --> 00:01:36.270
to be the ancestor of hundreds
of modern day languages,

00:01:36.270 --> 00:01:39.330
from English, to Spanish,
to German, Greek,

00:01:39.330 --> 00:01:41.520
Russian, Hindi, Persian, Norwegian,

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:43.050
the list goes on and on.

00:01:43.050 --> 00:01:45.810
Though this discovery is
archeological evidence

00:01:45.810 --> 00:01:48.360
that the Proto-Indo-Europeans drank wine,

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:52.020
historical linguists already
knew this to be the case.

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:52.853
How?

00:01:52.853 --> 00:01:55.650
Because many of these languages
still have similar words

00:01:55.650 --> 00:02:00.645
for the beverage, vino,
wijn, Wein, oinos, gini.

00:02:00.645 --> 00:02:03.030
Words that share a
common ancestor like this

00:02:03.030 --> 00:02:04.560
are known as cognates.

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:06.120
Linguists have even been able

00:02:06.120 --> 00:02:08.040
to reverse engineer these cognates

00:02:08.040 --> 00:02:10.080
to reconstruct what the ancestor word

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:12.390
might have sounded like: uoin.

00:02:12.390 --> 00:02:13.860
Though most people think of wine

00:02:13.860 --> 00:02:15.810
as the oldest alcoholic beverage,

00:02:15.810 --> 00:02:18.090
there's actually something older.

00:02:18.090 --> 00:02:21.240
Slightly before the Black
Sea Winery was in production,

00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:23.640
Neolithic peoples in
the Yellow River Valley

00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:25.260
were making an alcoholic drink

00:02:25.260 --> 00:02:27.840
out of fermented rice, fruit, and honey.

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:29.670
Honey may be the first ingredient

00:02:29.670 --> 00:02:32.460
that humans ever fermented into alcohol,

00:02:32.460 --> 00:02:34.860
because unlike grapes, rice, or wheat,

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:36.960
you don't need agriculture to harvest it,

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:39.600
as depicted by some
ancient cave paintings.

00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:42.120
The resulting beverage today called mead

00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:45.090
has a long list of cognates
that show we've been stealing

00:02:45.090 --> 00:02:47.400
from the bees for a very long time.

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:51.180
The Proto-Indo-European
ancestor of all these cognates,

00:02:51.180 --> 00:02:55.320
medu, may have been adapted
from the Proto-Semitic matak.

00:02:55.320 --> 00:02:58.230
From there, other non
Indo-European speakers

00:02:58.230 --> 00:03:00.330
likely borrowed the term as well.

00:03:00.330 --> 00:03:03.270
Hence, the Finnish
mesi, Proto-Chinese mit,

00:03:03.270 --> 00:03:04.710
and Japanese mitsu.

00:03:04.710 --> 00:03:06.390
That's a pretty prolific word

00:03:06.390 --> 00:03:09.570
considering you don't see
mead around much these days,

00:03:09.570 --> 00:03:12.510
unless you're one of those
people who brings it to parties.

00:03:12.510 --> 00:03:14.070
Moving to the beer aisle,

00:03:14.070 --> 00:03:17.310
a craft microbrew aficionado might be able

00:03:17.310 --> 00:03:20.700
to tell you the exact differences
between ales and lagers,

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:22.290
but the origins of those words

00:03:22.290 --> 00:03:24.993
are more like hefeweizen than a pilsner,

00:03:25.980 --> 00:03:27.750
in that it's cloudy and opaque.

00:03:27.750 --> 00:03:28.860
Bad beer joke.

00:03:28.860 --> 00:03:31.410
Ale is probably a Scandinavian term

00:03:31.410 --> 00:03:33.300
for a malt-based beverage.

00:03:33.300 --> 00:03:36.390
The word alu appears on
ancient runic inscriptions

00:03:36.390 --> 00:03:39.420
across Scandinavia, often
alongside references

00:03:39.420 --> 00:03:41.970
to leeks, suggesting that ale was thought

00:03:41.970 --> 00:03:43.740
to have medicinal properties.

00:03:43.740 --> 00:03:46.560
Some think the word is
related to the Latin alum,

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:50.220
meaning bitter, which would
make it a cognate with aluminum,

00:03:50.220 --> 00:03:52.950
or maybe it's connected
to the Germanic verb alan,

00:03:52.950 --> 00:03:55.140
which meant to grow or swell,

00:03:55.140 --> 00:03:58.110
perhaps a reference to
the fermentation process.

00:03:58.110 --> 00:04:00.210
This would make it cognates with old.

00:04:00.210 --> 00:04:02.670
Beer comes from the Old English beor,

00:04:02.670 --> 00:04:06.420
which some have suggested is
related to the word barley.

00:04:06.420 --> 00:04:08.130
This seems unlikely though,

00:04:08.130 --> 00:04:11.550
since beor was originally
a sweet, fruit-based drink.

00:04:11.550 --> 00:04:14.910
It wasn't until later that
it became a synonym for ale.

00:04:14.910 --> 00:04:16.740
The more accepted source is a borrowing

00:04:16.740 --> 00:04:19.440
of the Latin bibere, which meant to drink,

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:22.080
also where we get beverage and imbibe.

00:04:22.080 --> 00:04:24.570
Lager is short for the German lagerbier,

00:04:24.570 --> 00:04:26.550
which meant a beer that was laid down

00:04:26.550 --> 00:04:28.350
or stored for a long time.

00:04:28.350 --> 00:04:30.360
It comes from the P-I-E root legh,

00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:32.370
which also gives us the word law

00:04:32.370 --> 00:04:34.740
because you can lay down the law.

00:04:34.740 --> 00:04:37.140
Today, the term laid down is still used

00:04:37.140 --> 00:04:39.750
to describe the long
storage of alcoholic drinks.

00:04:39.750 --> 00:04:42.294
- I've got a few bottles
of the old Winyard left.

00:04:42.294 --> 00:04:44.662
It was laid down by my father.

00:04:44.662 --> 00:04:47.220
- I know you're not supposed
to follow beer with liquor,

00:04:47.220 --> 00:04:49.770
but let's move on to the hard stuff.

00:04:49.770 --> 00:04:52.680
The earliest archeological
evidence suggests

00:04:52.680 --> 00:04:54.870
that Muslim chemists were distilling wine

00:04:54.870 --> 00:04:58.350
into a concentrated liquor in
the eighth or ninth century.

00:04:58.350 --> 00:05:01.800
Alcohol actually has an
Arabic origin, al-kohl,

00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:05.010
a purified gray powder
used for cosmetic purposes.

00:05:05.010 --> 00:05:06.450
The practice spread to Europe

00:05:06.450 --> 00:05:08.100
where the distillation process was seen

00:05:08.100 --> 00:05:11.220
as extracting the very spirit of wine,

00:05:11.220 --> 00:05:13.740
which was believed to have
life-giving properties.

00:05:13.740 --> 00:05:17.400
Hence, the Latin name,
aqua vitae, water of life.

00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:19.980
This term migrated to
several other languages

00:05:19.980 --> 00:05:23.460
like eau de vie in French,
akvavit in Scandinavian,

00:05:23.460 --> 00:05:26.760
okowita in Polish, and
uisce beatha in Gaelic,

00:05:26.760 --> 00:05:29.280
which was eventually shortened to whiskey.

00:05:29.280 --> 00:05:30.780
Some linguists believe the term

00:05:30.780 --> 00:05:32.580
also influenced the Russian vodka,

00:05:32.580 --> 00:05:34.530
which literally means little water.

00:05:34.530 --> 00:05:37.680
As the distillation process
spread across the world,

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:39.630
the resulting liquors were typically named

00:05:39.630 --> 00:05:42.720
either after their main
ingredient, as in gin and rye,

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:45.420
or their place of origin,
like bourbon and scotch.

00:05:45.420 --> 00:05:47.130
The agave plant of Mexico is used

00:05:47.130 --> 00:05:50.460
to make three different
kinds of alcoholic drinks;

00:05:50.460 --> 00:05:53.520
tequila, which is named
after the town of origin,

00:05:53.520 --> 00:05:55.200
mezcal, which is named after the plant

00:05:55.200 --> 00:05:57.780
from the Nahuatl mexcalli, and pulque,

00:05:57.780 --> 00:06:00.540
a sour, milky beverage
made from agave sap.

00:06:00.540 --> 00:06:02.580
Pulque is the oldest of the three,

00:06:02.580 --> 00:06:05.310
originally called octli
by the Meso-Americans

00:06:05.310 --> 00:06:07.800
who invented it thousands of years ago.

00:06:07.800 --> 00:06:10.110
The Spanish name was probably a corruption

00:06:10.110 --> 00:06:12.870
of a Nahuatl term, poliuhqui octli,

00:06:12.870 --> 00:06:15.090
which means spoiled octli.

00:06:15.090 --> 00:06:17.910
Once you start mixing
liquors with other beverages,

00:06:17.910 --> 00:06:20.280
the result is widely known as a cocktail,

00:06:20.280 --> 00:06:22.650
although no one is certain why.

00:06:22.650 --> 00:06:25.920
One theory refers to a 19th
century New Orleans socialite

00:06:25.920 --> 00:06:28.200
who served mixed drinks in egg cups,

00:06:28.200 --> 00:06:30.510
known in French as coquetiers.

00:06:30.510 --> 00:06:33.630
Another suggests it's
named after a horse's tail

00:06:33.630 --> 00:06:35.910
that has been cut short,
as was the fashion

00:06:35.910 --> 00:06:37.830
with horses of mixed pedigree.

00:06:37.830 --> 00:06:39.690
Now that everyone has
the drink they ordered,

00:06:39.690 --> 00:06:42.390
it's time to raise our
glasses for a toast.

00:06:42.390 --> 00:06:45.300
Wait, why is it called a toast?

00:06:45.300 --> 00:06:46.890
It can't really have anything to do

00:06:46.890 --> 00:06:48.840
with twice cooked bread, can it?

00:06:48.840 --> 00:06:51.420
Actually, yes.

00:06:51.420 --> 00:06:54.150
There was an archaic tradition
of adding a small piece

00:06:54.150 --> 00:06:55.950
of charred bread into your wine

00:06:55.950 --> 00:06:58.470
to add flavor and soup acidity.

00:06:58.470 --> 00:07:01.110
Since this was typically
done on festive occasions,

00:07:01.110 --> 00:07:03.150
you can imagine it to
be the perfect moment

00:07:03.150 --> 00:07:04.320
to say something special

00:07:04.320 --> 00:07:06.360
before everyone takes their first sip.

00:07:06.360 --> 00:07:09.300
So whatever you're poison,
or no poison at all,

00:07:09.300 --> 00:07:14.163
salud, prost, zdarovya,
serefe, kanpai and cheers.

00:07:15.330 --> 00:07:18.330
Moving to the beer aisle,
a craft micro boot,

00:07:18.330 --> 00:07:21.600
micro brew, a craft micro (laughs).

00:07:21.600 --> 00:07:25.780
A craft mico, a craft mico. (sighs)

00:07:25.780 --> 00:07:26.613
Micro.

00:07:26.613 --> 00:07:30.463
Microwave, microbrew.
(slow soft music)

